WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.400
ok so for today's problem we've got this
compound here with a carbonyl and a

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.469
methyl group and our target molecule has
a methyl and a bromo group and what we

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:19.449
want to be able to do is figure out how
to get from here to here in two steps, give it a shot.

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.520
as we're analyzing this, let's take a look at the molecule

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.550
we're realizing that we have to lose oxygen, we
have to figure out a way to get that

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:44.260
oxygen atom off of there, and we also have to
figure out a way to get a bromine atom

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.559
on there, and so we have two steps to
play with, either one is gonna get rid of

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:51.320
the oxygen one is gonna add the bromine
or somehow both can happen at once but

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:54.800
what we definitely want to do is get rid of
that oxygen, so we don't really know of a

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.149
way to remove a carbonyl oxygen so why
don't we reduce it. let's take sodium

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.399
borohydride, just a nice soft reducing
agent, and let's as step 1 reduce the

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:08.990
carbonyl. we're gonna get an alcohol. now
that we have an alcohol we have some

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.810
different possibilities to think about.
now one possibility that we might wanna

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:18.100
use is some strong acid to
protonate that hydroxyl group, get it

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.280
to leave as a water molecule, but then what we want to

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.049
do is choose a strong acid selectively that
might perform the other transformation

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:32.270
that we also want to occur, so why don't we
use hydrobromic acid and see what happens.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.960
so once again the first step was that
we had some hydride and that went ahead

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.310
and reduced it so here is our alcohol,
now if we use hydrobromic acid

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:51.350
we're gonna get that right there, so the
hydroxyl has been protonated, now water

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:55.020
is a good leaving group, we are in acidic
conditions, so carbocations are going to be

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.119
fine, so that can go ahead and leave. now
the interesting thing here is if we have

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:03.970
a secondary carbocation, and remember
that a secondary carbocation that is

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.509
adjacent to a tertiary or quaternary
carbon, you're probably gonna get some

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:14.070
kind of hydride or or methanide shift.
and so what's happening here is we've

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:21.810
got this implied hydrogen and what we
can do is it'll spontaneously just do a

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:26.260
hydride shift and so if this hydride
moves over there that's going to

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.290
neutralize that carbocation, it's gonna leave a
carbocation at the tertiary position. once

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:35.980
again this happens because the tertiary carbocation is
more stable than a secondary carbocation, so that

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:40.030
is going to happen spontaneously and
pretty much instantly. and now since we

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.790
used hydrobromic acid, when this
deprotonated, its gonna leave a lot of

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.299
bromide swimming around in solution and
so that's going to kill two birds with

00:02:48.299 --> 00:02:52.870
one stone because if there's a lot of
bromide and one finds this carbocation

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.130
it is absolutely going to react and
generate the intended product. so because

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:02.360
we were clever in the selection of acid,
we've been able to induce this in two steps.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:07.360
the first thing we did was reduce that
ketone to the alcohol and then because

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.920
we used HBr we are able to get that
water to leave, allow this carbocation

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:17.190
rearrangement to occur spontaneously and
then the free bromide ion swimming

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.280
around in solution finds the carbocation and there we go, we get our product

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.879
thanks for watching guys, subscribe to my
channel for more tutorials and as always

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:47.999
feel free to email me

